findings.body:
When the fmterr recovery truncates the file, valid_up_to has already been advanced past every command the loader ran, including commands queued inside an open MULTI. The EOF path handles this at aof.c lines 1670-1674 by falling back to valid_before_multi. The new fmterr branch doesn't. So a tail like MULTI, SET a 1, then zero-filled bytes is truncated to keep the dangling MULTI and SET. The server starts without ever executing that transaction, but the file still contains it.

The server then appends new writes after the dangling MULTI. On the next restart those writes get queued into the same transaction. At EOF the loader reverts to valid_before_multi, so the write acknowledged after recovery is discarded. With aof-load-truncated no, the same state makes startup fail.

::diff{file="src/aof.c" lines="1724-1734"}

::callout{type="warning"}
This is the realistic power-loss case: a torn transaction followed by zero-filled blocks.

Fix: before truncating, if fakeClient->flags has CLIENT_MULTI set, use valid_before_multi as the cut point.

The argument reader reads len bytes and then reads two more bytes as the terminator without checking that they are CRLF (aof.c lines 1608-1622). If a write was torn and the bytes that follow are zero-filled, the loader can finish a command with garbage in its arguments. It then executes that command and advances valid_up_to (line 1661). The recovery truncates only the trailing garbage, so the mangled command stays in the file.

Example: a tail of an incomplete SET whose value length is 5, with only 3 real bytes, followed by NUL bytes. The loader consumes the 5 bytes as the value and the 2 NULs as the CRLF, runs SET foo with a corrupted value, then fails on the next line. Before this PR that file aborted without applying the SET.

The new Bad format test already shows this. It executes incr foc from a torn incr fo, and the test only checks the log line.

::code{file="src/aof.c" lines="1608-1622"}

::callout{type="security"}
Silent value corruption on the crash-recovery path. Validate the terminator before executing.

::diff{file="src/aof.c" lines="1727-1728"}

valid_up_to starts at 0 (aof.c line 1488). It is set only after an AOF command runs (line 1661). The RDB preamble branch (lines 1530-1554) loads the base but never sets valid_up_to to the post-preamble offset. If the first AOF command after a preamble is malformed, the fmterr branch computes size minus 0, which is the whole file, and truncates it to length 0. That deletes the preamble. The next restart starts empty.

The trigger is narrow: the file has to be under aof-load-broken-max-size (4 KiB by default), and it has to hold an RDB preamble followed by AOF commands. That includes the single-file appendonly.aof layout with aof-use-rdb-preamble. The same missing initialization already exists in the aof-load-truncated branch at line 1694, which truncates to 0 after a short read right after a preamble. This PR extends the problem to malformed bytes.

::code{file="src/aof.c" lines="1539-1553"}

::diff{file="src/aof.c" lines="1727-1728"}

truncate(valid_up_to) removes everything from the first malformed command to EOF, not just the bad bytes. If a corrupt byte sits in the middle of the file and valid writes follow it, every one of those valid writes is deleted, as long as the total stays under the limit. The redis.conf text says Redis truncates the corrupted portion (redis.conf lines 1532-1541), which suggests only the bad bytes go. With the 4096-byte default, up to 4 KiB of acknowledged writes can be dropped silently.

This may be an acceptable trade-off, but the docs should say that everything after the first bad byte is dropped. Alternatively, recovery could require that no parseable command follows the bad byte.

::diff{file="src/aof.c" lines="1727-1728"}
